# Collectibles as Investments

Collectibles include art, watches, jewelry, coins, stamps, wine, whiskey, and sports memorabilia. They offer enjoyment value alongside potential appreciation in niche markets, but are highly illiquid, subjective in value, and carry authenticity/fraud risk.

## Types
- Art & antiques: Paintings, sculptures, furniture
- Watches & jewelry: Luxury brands, precious metals
- Coins & stamps: Numismatics, philately
- Wine & whiskey: Rare vintages, aged spirits
- Sports memorabilia: Cards, autographs, equipment

## Tax Treatment
- Long-term gains taxed at maximum 28% (not favorable 20%)
- Short-term gains at ordinary rate
- No [[1031-exchange-strategy]] available (no like-kind deferral)

## Key Risks
- Highly illiquid
- No intrinsic value (subjective)
- Storage and insurance costs
- Authenticity and fraud risk
- No income or cash flow

## Recommended Allocation
0-10% of alternative investments (speculative portion).

